A purge that flushes must also retire

0041 routed the checkpoint through the replay batcher and, to keep the
completion drain safe, lifted the transaction off the checkpoint queue
first: with the transaction not at the head, no completion sees itself as
first-in-queue, and none of them advances the journal tail while the loop
is walking the very structure that would be freed. The tail advance was
left to "the purge loop re-inspects after this returns".

It does not, when it matters most. jbd_journal_alloc_block calls
jbd_journal_purge_cp_trans(flush=true, once=true) precisely because the
ring is full, and checks for free space the instant it returns. With
once=true the loop flushes the head transaction and breaks -- leaving it
on the queue, fully written, with the tail exactly where it was. No space
appeared, so the allocation failed, and a failed log allocation is
reported upward as ENOSPC.

The result was a journal that never drained. Sustained small-file work
fills a 1024-block ring in about a lap of 60 directories; from the first
lap onward every operation needing log space failed on a volume 17% full.
Measured on the fixture the mounted kill-recovery suite runs: 273 of 1800
operations refused with 2790 of 16384 blocks used and 911 of 4096 inodes
allocated. The pre-0041 build completes all 1800.

The damage did not stop at the refusals. A driver that cannot allocate log
space stops checkpointing, so the ring stays full of live transactions
across a crash -- and the recovery that follows finds a log whose records
run out mid-ring. The Linux kernel replays such an image to the same
inconsistent filesystem ours does; it is not recoverable by redo, and the
kill-recovery suite reported it as a recovery failure, which is where this
was found.

The fix restores what the old checkpoint did implicitly: after a
successful flush, re-inspect the same head transaction instead of
returning. It is now written in full, so the branch above retires it,
moves the tail, and stages the superblock -- and the caller finds the
space it asked for. Terminates for the same reason it always did: the
re-inspection takes the fully-written branch, which either frees the
transaction or stops at a recorded error.

Red first: 1800 operations through the default journal on a 64 MB volume,
which wraps the ring about fifteen times -- 273 refusals before, none
after, and e2fsck clean either way. The cell lives in
Tests/run_replay_speed_tests.sh ("1800 operations through a ring that
wraps ~15 times, none refused").

--- a/src/ext4_journal.c
+++ b/src/ext4_journal.c
@@ -2162,6 +2162,21 @@
 				if (jbd_journal_flush_trans(trans) != EOK)
 					break;
 				flushed = true;
+				/* The flush completed every buffer, but 0041
+				 * lifts the transaction off this queue for the
+				 * completion drain, so no completion saw itself
+				 * as head and none advanced the tail. Re-inspect
+				 * rather than returning: the transaction is now
+				 * fully written, and the branch above retires it
+				 * and moves the tail -- which is the free log
+				 * space this purge was called to produce. The
+				 * caller checks for that space immediately
+				 * (jbd_journal_alloc_block), so leaving the
+				 * retirement to some later purge meant a ring
+				 * that never drained: sustained small-file work
+				 * filled it and every later operation failed
+				 * with ENOSPC on a volume 17% full. */
+				continue;
 			}
 		}
 		if (once)
